  i think that  you know a lot of us thought lockdowns  probably were necessary right at the  start i certainly took that view  but i quite quickly began to realize as  professor mark woolhouse of edinburgh  university has spelled out in his book  the year the world went mad  that lockdowns are designed to eliminate  the virus  they don't work for simply um  controlling the spread of the virus  you know  they either  they can buy you a bit of time that's  for sure but  once you  realize that you can't eliminate the  virus in three  weeks or three months or something  then you've got to find a policy that  enables you to live with the virus for  two or three years and two or three  years of lockdowns are not acceptable  the  mental cost the physical cost the  health cost the economic cost on the  population is simply too high i think  we've learned that lesson i think we  were right to open up in july in 2021  and again not to lock down in december  2021 when most of the  public health experts were urging the  government to do so  yes we had an omicron wave in january  but it did not result in a significant  increase in deaths and hospitalizations  at least nothing like what was predicted  by some  and so um we are now in a position where  in the uk  and much of the rest of the west we can  get back to living normally while having  the occasional very bad cold um called  covid i just had that myself two weeks  ago um it's no fun but it's not uh  life-threatening and we have to be able  to to to live like that while of course  you know making special  efforts to protect the most vulnerable  people  well i'm glad you're feeling better matt  um  cindy uh matt touched on there sort of  the economic cost of this for china i'd  be interested to hear your thoughts on  how big do you think that will be i mean  we've seen some big cities like shanghai  and shenzhen going to lockdown recently  do you think china's gonna have a big  economic hit this year from the sort of  zero covered fallout  so earlier this month china had another  one of these chinese communist party  meetings called the two sessions and  there the vice pre the premier league  town announced china's gdp figure for  this year or target for this year 5.5  which sounds like a lot to us um but for  china that's the lowest in three decades  so the chinese authorities even you know  on the outset are making clear that  they're not expecting the economy to not  take a hit this year um and we also saw  last week presidency make this  commitment to more transparency in  regulation in the stock market which  made the stocks go up again because they  recognize that the economy needs a bit  of a boost but a lot of the costs we  just don't simply see you know we don't  see the aviation uh damage of you know  if you might think back to pre-clover  times the chinese were super  cosmopolitan you know because of  globalization they were traveling  everywhere there was trade going  everywhere a lot of that has gone now  you know someone like my mother going  home has to  do three pcr tests to get there and the  tickets are not easy to come by either  so that all of that will have an  economic cost and in china as well there  are 300 million informal workers people  without contracts who overnight can see  their jobs get lost um if they're  unlucky enough to be in a city who that  was locked down um overnight so all of  these things will have costs but i think  china  thinks that with a zero cover policy if  it's one city at a time that goes down  and then they normally expect that  within a month at least with delta you  could get rid of those local infections  omicron it might take longer at least  that means other cities can still thrive  in the meantime you know this kind of  community infection would they believe  have more disruption and i guess you  know zero covert has you can criticize  it for many reasons but on saving lives  if that is your only narrow metric it is  doing quite well  do you agree with that matt has it been  a success in that sense  yes i think you can't deny that uh  draconian lockdowns have saved lives um  i think there's only really two policies  that have saved lives during this  pandemic one is locked dance and the  other is vaccines  i don't myself believe that masks and  school closures and other you know many  of the other things have worked well or  indeed you know  disinfecting our hands etc we now know  that wasn't necessarily good advice i  think it's worth  it's worth just being a bit humble here  this virus has been humiliating for  um  a lot of people it's been humiliating  for public health uh advice often not  necessarily getting it right early on or  even later on during the pandemic it's  been humiliating for politicians in  terms of what measures they can take etc  and as cindy says it's been humiliating  for a  huge nation that was opening up to the  world and has now had to turn itself  back into a bit of a hermit kingdom  which cannot be good for the world um it  is cutting chinese people off from  uh the rest of the world that's bad for  them and bad for us um  much as the war is doing for russia  so it does feel like a very different  world from five years ago  that we're now living in with these um  rather dramatic balkanizations of  the the global body politic  that's a really interesting point and  cindy i know xi jinping states a lot of  his international reputation on how well  china handled the virus the beginning of  the pandemic is that starting to sort of  crumble away a bit now and affect his  own leadership do you think  yeah i mean  there are people within china who are  ever so slightly starting to crack  through you know this this veneer of  zero-covered um zeitgeist you know  people are able to now come out and say  well maybe we should think about the  next steps and the reason china has been  so slow on doing that is exactly as you  say john that xi jinping has made this  part of his political legacy after the  initial humiliation of the wuhan  outbreak she was saying you know we've  got the ppe we've got the lockdowns and  we've got the vaccine diplomacy well on  all three of those things there are  things that criticisms one can make of  it and especially on the zerekova policy  it's become such a political point that  you can't help but realize that the  politicians in china are not following  the science  and are really just you know embarrassed  to turn around and they're realizing  that they're too slow intriguingly we  are seeing signs that  there is more allowance of discussion of  the next steps so for example one  shanghai epidemiologist jamming hong  this time last year said that we might  want to live with kovet he got  absolutely pilloried online  these days when he says some of  something similar you know people online  are allowed to say that you know have  more diverse views so you do wonder the  changing position of censors in that  respect and i think china hopes that  lockdowns for it has been a time buying  mechanism as matt was saying and it  hopes that by the end of this year they  can develop its own mrna vaccines and  that it can have very good anti-virals  which it has already approved a few of  including from pfizer actually  under emergency approvals so it's hoping  that it can medicate its way out of the  situation with better medication than  just its vaccines at the moment um and  until then it just has to basically buy  time and the question is does that still  work with omicron  and that what cost because the cost is  getting ever higher the more contagious  the variants become  i mean as a final point you mentioned in  your piece that the west should perhaps  be doing more and sending millions of  doses of our vaccines to china um i  guess it comes down to a question of  pride do you think china will accept  that gift  um  i think it's unlikely i think um  the the  chinese pride is very involved here and  the  it it would have to be a perfectly  commercial arrangement look we've got  vaccines now we probably don't need to  put forth boosters into most  people's arms uh in the west um why  won't you  take these  there has been such a strong tendency in  chinese biotech in particular  to  see western technology as something you  want to learn from you want to emulate  and you then want to  replace with your own technology that i  think it isn't an easy moment for the  chinese government to say  actually please could you help us  but it would be  sensible i think if they did that um and  just another point if i may uh  if  um  you know we cannot open up again to  travel from china for a long time  that will prevent chinese scientists  particularly from wuhan  coming to the west and  giving us useful bits of information  that might enable us to solve the  enduring puzzle of where this virus came  from and how it jumped into human beings  in the first place something we still  haven't solved and we're getting very  very little cooperation from the chinese  government in tackling  thank you matt and thank you cindy  and if you want to listen to cindy's  podcast you can find chinese whispers  and all the usual podcast platforms or  at spectator dot code uk forward slash  whispers  that's it for this week  once again we're delighted that  canaccord genuity wealth management is  sponsoring the week in 60 minutes  cold will provide the expertise you need  to build your wealth with confidence you  can visit candu wealth.com to find out  more  and if you enjoyed this episode don't  forget to subscribe to the spectators  youtube channel just click the subscribe  button at the bottom of this video and  tap the bell icon so you never miss an  episode  thanks for watching and do join us again  next week  [Music]  you
 

